<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Monash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir John Monash was an Australian civil engineer and general. After commanding a brigade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> and a division in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he led the Australian Corps from May 1918, planning the battle of Hamel and leading his corps in the Allied offensives of the last months of the war, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> to the Hindenburg Line. He is regarded as one of the war's ablest commanders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-monash.jpg" alt="John Monash" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Monash_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Monash was born in West Melbourne to Jewish parents, Louis Monash and Bertha Manasse, both from Krotoschin in the Prussian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a>; the family spoke German at home. He spent part of his boyhood at Jerilderie in New South Wales, where his father ran a store, before the family returned to Melbourne in 1877. At Scotch College he passed the matriculation examination at 14 and was dux at 16, and he later took engineering, arts and law degrees at the University of Melbourne.</p><p>As a civil engineer he worked on bridge and railway construction and did much to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">reinforced concrete</a> to Australian practice, through partnerships with J. T. N. Anderson and later the Reinforced Concrete &amp; Monier Pipe Construction Co. He also rose through the militia from 1884, commanding the 13th Infantry Brigade from 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaign</a> his 4th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> held the line between Pope's Hill and Courtney's Post, where the valley behind became known as Monash Valley. In the August offensive its attack towards Hill 971 failed amid poor maps, heavy resistance and mountainous ground, and he was criticised for excessive delegation; by mid-August the brigade was down to 1,400 of its 3,350 men.</p><p>On the Western Front he trained the 3rd Division in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with attention to detail and led it at Messines, Broodseinde and the First Battle of Passchendaele. His appointment to the Australian Corps was opposed by Charles Bean and Keith Murdoch, who favoured Brudenell White; Bean later called his own intervention ill-judged. Monash stressed thorough planning, the combined use of infantry, artillery, tanks and aircraft, and the welfare of his troops, arranging hot meals for the front line at Hamel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war Monash directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> of Australian troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, then headed the State Electricity Commission of Victoria from 1920 and was vice-chancellor of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> from 1923. He became president of the Zionist Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1927 and helped plan the Shrine of Remembrance. He died of a heart attack on 8 October 1931; an estimated 300,000 mourners attended his state funeral. His face is on the Australian $100 note, and a proposal to promote him posthumously to field marshal was rejected in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Monash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The landing of the Australian and New Zealand Army Corps at Gallipoli on 25 April 1915, commemorated as Anzac Day, became a founding national legend, and the Australian Corps under Sir <b>John Monash</b> fought with distinction on the Western Front in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-37.81,144.96&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Melbourne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Monash"><image href="/maps/globes/g150s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4660 5674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4660 5674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-37.81,144.96&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
